# Unsure about foritself. S3 says those who desire virtue "for its own sake" desire it because being conscious of it is a pleasure.
# In ordinary usage that could read as desiring virtue for the pleasure rather than for itself, which would differ from S2's "desired for itself".
# The text uses the phrase alike in S2-S4, so I kept it as one sense.
# Similarly, "part of happiness" in A1 (via pleasure and pain) fits the text's equation of happiness with pleasure, so I named no bridge.
